Linear Regression :

-> Minimum Sample Requirement :
For a dataset with m features, we generally need at least (m + 1) independent samples to uniquely determine a linear regression model.
Reason :
A linear regression model in m-dimensional space is written as:
y = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + ... + wₘxₘ + b
Here, there are (m + 1) unknown parameters:
    • m weights : (w₁, w₂, ..., wₘ)
    • 1 bias/intercept : (b)
To uniquely solve for these unknown parameters, we require at least (m + 1) independent samples.
Example :
Simple Linear Regression:
y = mx + c
This model has 2 unknown parameters:
    • slope (m)
    • intercept (c)
Therefore, we need at least 2 distinct points to determine a unique regression line.
Case:
    • 2 points  → One unique line can be drawn.
    • 1 point   → Infinitely many lines can pass through the point.
Important Note :
(m + 1) samples represent only the minimum mathematical requirement for a unique solution.
In practical machine learning, we usually use far more than (m + 1) samples so that the model learns general patterns and avoids overfitting.
